      "raw_response": " ANSWER: SUPPORTED\n\nThe abstract suggests that genomic aberrations in metastases can provide information for targeted therapy, as it demonstrates the existence of shared mutations between primary carcinomas and their matched metastases. These shared mutations can aid in understanding the clonal evolution of metastasis and provide a realistic model for the testing of novel therapies (as stated in the last sentence).\n\nThe study uses whole-exome sequencing of 103 matched benign, malignant, and metastatic skin tumors from genetically heterogeneous mice, which allows for the identification of somatic genetic alterations that are specifically related to initiation of carcinogenesis by Hras or Kras mutations. This information could potentially be used for targeted therapy.\n\nReference: Nature Genetics (2016) - Evolution of metastasis revealed by mutational landscapes of chemically induced skin cancers. https://www.nature.com/articles/ng.3628",
